				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2025-03-06T18:18:44.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> There's certainly a benefit of a consistent labelling scheme across repos, so someone looking at a new-to-them issue list doesn't need to first learn a labelling taxonomy.</p><p>Pulling the bug vs feature issue type to the front of the description also helps replace people's custom schemes of using an emoji to start an issue name.</p><p>But I suspect the driving factor for GitHub may have been easier integration into their more complex project management features (milestones, versions, etc).</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
